# Trivy waivers — pgvector image only (wired to the pgvector scan step in
# .github/workflows/ci.yml via the `trivyignores:` input; NOT applied to the
# neo4j or ollama scans, which stay strict).
#
# Context: the relational store is pinned to pgvector/pgvector:0.8.5-pg17-trixie
# (published 2026-07-08, pinned 2026-07-31 at 23 days old — a clean §6.7 image
# soak). trixie (Debian 13.4) is the cleanest base pgvector ships; there is no
# UBI variant.
#
# Everything waived below is a BUNDLED dependency — the Go stdlib compiled into
# the `gosu` binary the image ships. Bundled CVEs are identical across base
# variants and fixable only by an upstream rebuild, which is exactly the class
# §6.7 allows waiving (the same shape as the neo4j netty/jetty waivers).
#
# HISTORY — the two unusual things about the OUTGOING 0.8.2 pin, both now resolved:
#
#   1. AGE BEND (spec §6.7, "no exceptions without explicit conversation").
#      0.8.2-pg17-trixie was 6 days old when pinned on 2026-05-21, inside the
#      7-day image soak — taken by explicit user decision because it was a
#      CVE-*fix* release (Issue #4). RESOLVED: the 0.8.5 pin needed no bend.
#
#   2. OS-PACKAGE CVEs WERE WAIVED HERE (atypical — normally we *fix* OS CVEs by
#      moving to a fresher rebuild, never waive them). That exception was forced
#      by there being no fresher rebuild to move to: every pgvector tag had been
#      rebuilt 2026-05-15, and going older was strictly worse. RESOLVED 2026-07-31:
#      the 0.8.5 rebuild ships Debian's fixed packages, so all eleven OS entries
#      were dropped (see the section below) and the normal fix-don't-waive rule
#      applies again.
#
# All waivers across images are also indexed in infra/trivy/WAIVERS.md (the
# review register: fixed-in versions + drop conditions, checked periodically).
#
# Revisit: every pgvector tag bump. Drop the gosu/stdlib entries when upstream
# rebuilds gosu on a patched Go toolchain. If an OS-package CVE ever reappears,
# FIX it with a fresher tag — re-waiving is the exception, not the default.

# ───────────────────────── OS packages (Debian 13.4) ─────────────────────────
# NONE — all eleven OS-package waivers were DROPPED on 2026-07-31 by the
# 0.8.2-pg17-trixie → 0.8.5-pg17-trixie bump (Grzymalin S5). Nine of them the
# rebuild genuinely fixed, by shipping Debian's patched packages:
#   * gnutls    CVE-2026-42010, CVE-2026-33845, CVE-2026-33846, CVE-2026-3833,
#               CVE-2026-42009                  (→ 3.8.9-3+deb13u4)
#   * krb5      CVE-2026-40355, CVE-2026-40356  (→ 1.21.3-5+deb13u1)
#   * openssl   CVE-2026-45447                  (→ 3.5.6-1~deb13u2)
#   * libcap2   CVE-2026-4878                   (→ 1:2.75-10+deb13u1)
# The other two — CVE-2026-42011 (gnutls) and CVE-2026-29111 (systemd) — were
# already STALE: a raw scan of the *outgoing* 0.8.2 pin no longer reported them,
# so they had been waiving nothing. Removed in the same pass rather than carried.
# Verified by diffing raw Trivy HIGH/CRITICAL findings (no ignore file) across
# both tags: the bump clears these and introduces nothing new.
#
# This closes the "OS-PACKAGE CVEs ARE WAIVED HERE" exception described in note 2
# of the header above. The normal rule applies again: OS CVEs get FIXED by moving
# to a fresher rebuild, never waived. Restore an OS waiver only if that situation
# recurs — no fresher rebuild exists — and say so explicitly.

# ─── REOPENED 2026-08-17: the situation recurred. Saying so explicitly. ───
#
# The rule above requires that re-waiving an OS CVE be justified by "no fresher
# rebuild exists". That is now the case, and it was VERIFIED, not assumed: the
# newest pgvector tag of any age — 0.8.6-pg17-trixie (pub 2026-08-13) — was pulled
# and scanned, and it still ships util-linux 2.41-5, carrying CVE-2026-53615
# unchanged. The bookworm variant was checked too and is strictly worse: it carries
# the same CVE with status "affected" (Debian 12 has published NO fix at all) plus
# ~14 further unfixed HIGHs in perl/ncurses/libxml2/zlib. It only *appears* clean
# under --ignore-unfixed, which hides every CVE the distro has not patched — a
# scanner artifact, not a security improvement. trixie remains the right base.
#
# So there is no fresher rebuild to move to, exactly as in the Issue #4 situation
# that opened this exception the first time. These two entries are time-boxed by
# the drop conditions recorded against each; both are also in infra/trivy/WAIVERS.md.

# CVE-2026-53615 — util-linux family, 9 packages (bsdutils, libblkid1,
# liblastlog2-2, libmount1, libsmartcols1, libuuid1, login, mount, util-linux)
# (HIGH): integer overflow/wraparound in libblkid/src/partitions/dos.c — the DOS
# partition-table parser. Reached only by code that PROBES A BLOCK DEVICE's
# partition table (blkid/lsblk/mount against untrusted media or a disk image).
# Postgres does none of that: it reads a filesystem path inside a Docker volume,
# and nothing in this container probes an attacker-supplied block device. The
# container is 127.0.0.1-bound, non-root, single trusted user. Unreachable here.
# Fixed in Debian 13 as 2.41.5-0+deb13u1 — published, but not yet present in ANY
# pgvector image build (0.8.6 verified above).
# Drop when a pgvector rebuild ships util-linux >= 2.41.5-0+deb13u1.
CVE-2026-53615
# CVE-2026-6473 — postgresql-17 / postgresql-client-17 (HIGH, CVSS 8.8): integer
# wraparound in multiple PostgreSQL server features lets an unprivileged database
# user undersize an allocation and write out of bounds, potentially executing code
# as the OS user running the database.
#
# FALSE POSITIVE — this is a version-comparison artifact, not an exposure. The
# upstream PostgreSQL advisory (postgresql.org/support/security/CVE-2026-6473/,
# read 2026-08-17) lists the fix as landing in 18.4 / **17.10** / 16.14 / 15.18 /
# 14.23, published 2026-05-14. This image ships PostgreSQL **17.10** (verified:
# `postgres --version` → "17.10 (Debian 17.10-1.pgdg13+1)"), so the running code
# ALREADY CONTAINS THE FIX. Trivy reports it because our build comes from the PGDG
# apt repo (17.10-1.pgdg13+1) while Trivy matches Debian's own security tracker,
# whose fixed version is 17.11-0+deb13u1 — and 17.10-1.pgdg13+1 sorts below that.
# Waived only to clear the gate, NOT because a real vulnerability is being accepted.
# Drop at the 0.8.6-pg17-trixie bump (ships PostgreSQL 17.11, verified; tag pub
# 2026-08-13, clears the 7-day image soak on 2026-08-21), which makes it disappear.
CVE-2026-6473

# ──────────────── Bundled dependency: gosu Go stdlib (gobinary) ───────────────
# gosu is a setuid step-down wrapper: it drops root and exec()s Postgres. It
# opens no sockets, makes no TLS connection, and parses no URLs, archives, or
# mail — so every one of these stdlib CVEs sits in unreachable code. Identical
# across all base variants; fixable only by an upstream gosu rebuild on a patched
# Go toolchain. Same class as the neo4j netty waiver.

# CVE-2025-68121 — crypto/tls (CRITICAL): incorrect cert validation on TLS
# session resumption. gosu makes no TLS connections.
CVE-2025-68121
# CVE-2025-58183 — archive/tar (HIGH): unbounded allocation parsing GNU sparse map.
CVE-2025-58183
# CVE-2025-61726 — net/url (HIGH): memory exhaustion in query-parameter parsing.
CVE-2025-61726
# CVE-2025-61728 — archive/zip (HIGH): excessive CPU building archive index.
CVE-2025-61728
# CVE-2025-61729 — crypto/x509 (HIGH): DoS via crafted certificate.
CVE-2025-61729
# CVE-2026-25679 — net/url (HIGH): insufficient host/authority component validation in
# url.Parse (accepts certain invalid URLs that should be rejected). gosu parses no URLs.
CVE-2026-25679
# CVE-2026-32280 — crypto/x509 (HIGH): DoS in certificate-chain building.
CVE-2026-32280
# CVE-2026-32281 — crypto/x509 (HIGH): DoS via inefficient chain validation.
CVE-2026-32281
# CVE-2026-32283 — crypto/tls (HIGH): DoS via multiple TLS 1.3 key updates.
CVE-2026-32283
# CVE-2026-33811 — net (HIGH): cgo DNS LookupCNAME on long CNAME triggers double-free
# of C memory + crash (higher severity than DoS-only). gosu opens no sockets, runs no
# DNS lookups.
CVE-2026-33811
# CVE-2026-33814 — net/http2 (HIGH): infinite loop on crafted SETTINGS frames.
CVE-2026-33814
# CVE-2026-39820 — net/mail (HIGH): crafted input to ParseAddress / ParseAddressList.
CVE-2026-39820
# CVE-2026-39836 — net (HIGH): panic in Dial/LookupPort on NUL byte (Windows).
CVE-2026-39836
# CVE-2026-42499 — net/mail (HIGH): DoS via consumePhrase on pathological input.
CVE-2026-42499

# ─── Second wave (disclosed 2026-05-21 → 2026-05-26, same gosu binary) ───
# Same reachability story: gosu opens no sockets, parses no URLs / mail /
# archives / certificates, and does no TLS — so every one of these sits in
# unreachable code. Fixable only by an upstream gosu rebuild on a patched Go
# toolchain (Go 1.24.8+ / 1.25.2+ cover most; -61727 needs Go 1.24.11 / 1.25.5).
# Triggered the security-CI failure on PR #18.

# CVE-2025-47912 — net/url (HIGH): Parse permits non-IPv6 in bracketed-host form.
CVE-2025-47912
# CVE-2025-58185 — encoding/asn1 (HIGH): unbounded allocation parsing DER payload.
CVE-2025-58185
# CVE-2025-58186 — net/http (HIGH): default 1MB header limit can be bypassed.
CVE-2025-58186
# CVE-2025-58187 — crypto/x509 (HIGH): inefficient name-constraint check → DoS.
CVE-2025-58187
# CVE-2025-58188 — crypto/x509 (HIGH): DSA-public-key cert chain validation issue.
CVE-2025-58188
# CVE-2025-58189 — crypto/tls (HIGH): ALPN handshake error path information leak.
CVE-2025-58189
# CVE-2025-61723 — encoding/pem (HIGH): non-linear parse time on invalid PEM inputs;
# untrusted PEM data exhausts CPU. gosu parses no PEM data.
CVE-2025-61723
# CVE-2025-61724 — net/textproto (HIGH): Reader.ReadResponse response-building DoS.
CVE-2025-61724
# CVE-2025-61725 — net/mail (HIGH): ParseAddress domain-literal construction.
CVE-2025-61725
# CVE-2025-61727 — crypto/x509 (HIGH): excluded-subdomain constraint not enforced.
CVE-2025-61727

# ─── Third wave (disclosed 2026-05-26 → 2026-05-27, same gosu binary) ───
# Same reachability story as waves 1 + 2 reframed for these specific code paths:
# gosu is a setuid step-down wrapper that drops root and exec()s Postgres — it
# does not render HTML templates and does not run an HTTP server (let alone a
# reverse proxy). All three fixed in Go 1.25.10 / 1.26.3 (the same Go release
# that resolves five of the wave-2 entries); only an upstream gosu rebuild on
# a patched Go toolchain clears them.
#
# **Threshold tripped.** The WAIVERS.md pattern note (added 2026-05-26 after
# wave 2) recorded a "build our own image if this happens a third time" rule.
# Wave 3 trips it. Evaluation tracked in Issue #22 — this waiver extension is
# the *unblock* (so PR #21's CI can go green), not the resolution. Triggered
# the security-CI failure on PR #21.
#
# **Codex P2 correction (2026-05-27):** an earlier draft of these comments
# mis-attributed CVE-2026-39823 to `net/url` and justified it via "gosu parses
# no URLs". The actual CVE is in `html/template` (XSS via URLs inside a `<meta>`
# tag's content attribute), and the justification has to match the real code
# path. Rationale below now matches NVD + Go vuln-DB GO-2026-4982.

# CVE-2026-39823 — html/template (HIGH): URLs are not correctly escaped inside
# `<meta>` tag content attributes; ASCII whitespace around `=` bypasses the
# escaper, enabling XSS in rendered HTML. gosu renders no HTML templates, so
# the vulnerable code path is never executed.
CVE-2026-39823
# CVE-2026-39825 — net/http/httputil (HIGH): ReverseProxy forwards query
# parameters not visible to the configured Rewrite function once ParseQuery's
# parameter limit is exceeded. gosu runs no HTTP server, let alone a reverse
# proxy.
CVE-2026-39825
# CVE-2026-39826 — html/template (HIGH): incorrect data escaping when a
# `<script>` tag has an empty or whitespace-only `type` attribute, enabling
# XSS. gosu renders no HTML templates, so the vulnerable code path is never
# executed.
CVE-2026-39826
# CVE-2026-42504 — mime (HIGH): quadratic complexity in WordDecoder.DecodeHeader
# (CWE-407) — a crafted MIME header with many invalid encoded-words consumes
# excessive CPU (DoS). gosu decodes no MIME headers and takes no untrusted input,
# so the vulnerable path is never executed. (GO-2026-5038.)
CVE-2026-42504

# ─── Fifth wave (surfaced by the Trivy DB 2026-06-23, same gosu binary) ───
# CVE-2026-27145 — crypto/x509 (HIGH): `(*x509.Certificate).VerifyHostname` called
# `matchHostnames` such that hostname verification could be incorrect. Fixed in Go
# 1.25.11 / 1.26.4. gosu opens no TLS connections and verifies no certificates, so
# VerifyHostname is never called — the vulnerable path is unreachable. Only an
# upstream gosu rebuild on a patched Go toolchain clears it (same as waves 1–4).
CVE-2026-27145

# ─── Sixth wave (surfaced by the Trivy DB 2026-07-13, same gosu binary) ───
# CVE-2026-39822 — os (HIGH): the Go 1.24 `os.Root` API improperly follows a
# symlink out of the confined root when the final path component is a symlink and
# the path ends in "/" (`root.Open("symlink/")` escapes) → directory traversal.
# CVSS 7.8, attack vector LOCAL (AV:L) — needs a local actor to plant the symlink
# and induce the process to open it via os.Root. gosu is a setuid step-down
# wrapper that drops root and exec()s Postgres; it opens no untrusted paths through
# os.Root, and this deployment is 127.0.0.1-bound, single trusted user, non-root —
# so the vulnerable path is unreachable. Fixed in Go 1.25.12 / 1.26.5; gosu ships
# Go 1.24.6. Only an upstream gosu rebuild on a patched Go toolchain clears it.
CVE-2026-39822

# ─── Seventh wave (surfaced by the Trivy DB 2026-08-15, same gosu binary) ───
# Reddened the scheduled `main` run three nights running (08-15, 08-16, 08-17)
# against an unchanged pin — pure treadmill, nothing in the repo moved.
#
# All seven are one Go-toolchain advisory batch, fixed in Go 1.25.13 / 1.26.6 /
# 1.27.0-rc.3; gosu still ships Go v1.24.6. CONFIRMED unfixable by any tag, not
# assumed: 0.8.6-pg17-trixie (pub 2026-08-13, the newest tag of any age) was pulled
# and scanned and carries the identical 22-CVE gosu set. Only an upstream gosu
# rebuild on a patched Go toolchain clears them.
#
# Reachability is the same story as waves 1-6, and it is the whole justification:
# gosu is a setuid step-down wrapper that drops root and exec()s Postgres. It opens
# no sockets, serves no HTTP, makes no TLS connection, renders no templates, and
# parses no XML, URLs, ASN.1 or Punycode. Every path below is compiled in but never
# executed. The container is additionally 127.0.0.1-bound, non-root, single trusted
# user. The SAME seven-CVE batch also hits the ollama binary — see wave 10 in
# infra/trivy/ollama.trivyignore (six of the seven; -39821 was already waived there).

# CVE-2026-33818 — encoding/asn1 (HIGH): DoS via excessive recursion in Unmarshal
# on a crafted DER payload. gosu parses no ASN.1.
CVE-2026-33818
# CVE-2026-39821 — golang.org/x/net/idna, vendored into net/http (HIGH): privilege
# escalation via incorrect Punycode label processing of internationalized hostnames.
# Trivy attributes it to `stdlib` because Go vendors x/net/idna into the standard
# library. gosu resolves no hostnames and makes no outbound request.
CVE-2026-39821
# CVE-2026-56853 — net/http (HIGH): unencrypted HTTP/2 (h2c) connections are
# vulnerable to DoS. gosu runs no HTTP server or client.
CVE-2026-56853
# CVE-2026-56858 — html/template (HIGH): XSS via pathological input. gosu renders
# no HTML templates.
CVE-2026-56858
# CVE-2026-56859 — encoding/xml (HIGH): DoS via an XML decoding recursion-depth
# issue. gosu parses no XML.
CVE-2026-56859
# CVE-2026-56860 — net/url (HIGH): DoS from quadratic complexity in path handling.
# gosu parses no URLs.
CVE-2026-56860
# CVE-2026-56862 — crypto/tls (HIGH): DoS via indefinite KeyUpdate messages. gosu
# makes no TLS connection.
CVE-2026-56862
